3DGBA's wireless Game Boy Advance link (the "local termination" trade path,
source/celiolink.c / celiolink.h / celiolink_payloads.h and the state-F driver
in source/gbacore.c) is a clean-room reimplementation of the technique
pioneered by the Celio-Link project, which brings the Pokemon Generation III
games online via a USB dongle + relay server.

The specific ideas adopted from Celio-Link:
  * LOCAL TERMINATION of the Gen-3 link: the device IS the link partner and
    answers every SIO transfer locally at full speed, so the game never waits
    on the network. Only semantic data (the party block, trainer/LinkPlayer
    block, and the trade select/confirm decisions) crosses the wire.
  * The PacketLayer framing: the single-word handshake, the [CRC][8 command]
    9-word command frames, and the additive mutual checksum (seed 0xB9A0, reset
    per command block).
  * The TransiveStruct {init, transive, transiveDone} command-handler dispatch
    and the trade-section state machine (LinkPlayer / party / select / confirm
    / close), modeled on Celio's emu-module sections and callbacks.

Files studied (reference only — no Celio source is vendored here):
  Celio-Firmware: src/layers/packetLayer.*, src/callbacks/* (TransiveStruct,
  blockCommand, sendLinkTypeCommand, moveCommand, readyExit/CloseLink,
  usbLinkCommand), src/sections/tradeConnection.cpp, tradeSetup.cpp,
  src/link_defines.h; plus the Celio-Server (relay model) and Celio-Client
  (transport-emitter seam, the model for 3DGBA's future online backend).

The 3DGBA design notes derived from this study live in docs/kb/celio/.

